(2) Prevention efforts as referred to in paragraph (1) are implemented by means of:
a. building networks and collaboration with law enforcement apparatus, government apparatus, as well as educational institutions, health facilities, hospitals, various NGOs that care about women and children;
b. building community and family participation and awareness toward the prevention and protection of women and children from acts of violence;
c. establishing a violence prevention system that provides a complete and easily accessible information system;
d. conducting socialization regarding laws and regulations related to the protection of women and children victims of violence;
e. providing critical education about the rights of women and children as victims of violence to the community; and 2021, No. 3 -83-
f. opening complaint posts for the protection of women and children from acts of violence.

Article 25
1. The PUG Focal Point in each SKPD consists of officials and/or staff whose field covers planning and/or program duties.
2021, No. 3 -87-
2. The PUG Focal Point as referred to in paragraph (1), has the duties to:
a. promote gender mainstreaming in the work unit;
b. facilitate the preparation of work plans and budgeting of the SKPD that are Gender responsive;
c. conduct training, socialization, advocacy for gender mainstreaming to all officials and staff within the SKPD environment;
d. encourage the implementation of gender analysis on policies, programs, and activities in the work unit; and
e. facilitate the preparation of gender data in each SKPD;
3. The PUG Focal Point as referred to in paragraph (1), is selected and determined by the head/leader of the SKPD.

Article 6
(1) The obligations of Regional Government in efforts to protect women include:
a. establishing and implementing policy programs and activities for the protection of women's rights;
b. conducting cooperation with the community and/or Community Organizations in the implementation of women's protection in accordance with laws and regulations;
c. conducting rehabilitation and empowerment of women who are victims of acts of violence;
2021, No. 3 -80-
d. providing special protection to women victims of acts of violence and human trafficking; and
e. taking preventive action in order to prevent violations of women's rights.
(2) The obligations of Regional Government in efforts to protect children include:
a. being responsible for respecting the fulfillment of children's rights without distinguishing ethnicity, religion, race, class, gender, ethnic group, culture and language, legal status, birth order, and physical and/or mental condition;
b. having the obligation and responsibility to implement and support national policy in the implementation of child protection in the region;
c. policy as referred to in letter b can be realized through regional efforts to build child-friendly regencies/cities;
d. having the obligation and responsibility to provide support for facilities, infrastructure, and availability of human resources in the implementation of child protection;
e. guaranteeing the protection, care, and welfare of children by taking into account the rights and obligations of parents, guardians, or other persons who are legally responsible for the child;
f. supervising the implementation of child protection.
g. guaranteeing children to use their rights in expressing opinions in accordance with the age and level of intelligence of the child.

